{
  "mechanism": "Replace in-sample least-squares Monte Carlo with cross-fitted backward induction. The parent estimates continuation and makes stopping decisions on the same 256 paths, so regression noise can systematically distort the exercise boundary. Out-of-fold continuation estimates make each training-path decision independent of that path’s current regression residual, while ridge shrinkage controls small-sample variance.",
  "implementation": "Create deterministic 8-fold splits using the supplied seed. Work backward from maturity. At each exercise date, construct a compact standardized basis from log-prices, immediate payoff, cross-sectional mean/min/max, dispersion, and low-order payoff interactions. For each fold, fit ridge regression on the other seven folds against their currently realized future cash flows and predict continuation only for the held-out fold. Choose the ridge penalty from a small fixed grid by inner generalized-cross-validation, with a modest positive floor such as 1e-4. Update every path’s cash flow using only its out-of-fold prediction; exercise only when immediate payoff is positive and exceeds continuation. After all out-of-fold decisions at that date are fixed, fit one full-sample ridge model to the pre-decision future-cash-flow targets and store it for prediction. Clip its continuation predictions to a robust, date-specific range derived from training targets, such as the 1st and 99th percentiles. Preserve the parent program’s file interface and use only vectorized NumPy linear algebra.",
  "expected_behavior": "Cross-fitting should reduce optimistic and unstable continuation estimates near the stopping boundary, improving genuinely out-of-sample exercise timing rather than merely increasing training payoff. Ridge regularization and clipping should reduce extreme decisions caused by nearly collinear features. Eight folds retain 224 observations per regression and require only a small number of low-dimensional solves, fitting comfortably within 30 seconds.",
  "failure_condition": "The intervention can lose when folds contain too few relevant in-the-money observations, when path dependence is not represented by the current state, or when continuation is strongly nonlinear and the compact ridge basis is misspecified. It should be rejected if seeded validation shows lower discounted payoff across multiple folds or excessive sensitivity to fold assignment; a prior candidate score alone is not evidence that cross-fitting caused improvement."
}
